From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 21:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] lockscreen: log the configured surface geometry

The lock screen is reported from the machine as visibly lower resolution than
the greeter. The panel is confirmed 3840x2160 (card0-DP-2 native mode, read
from sysfs on the O6). Patch 0011 added buffer-scale handling on
the assumption that a missing scale was the cause. It is not: a read-only
wl_output probe run against the live session measured

    OUTPUT SCALE = 1

so nothing is being upscaled and 0011, while correct in general, is inert on
this hardware. The cause is still unknown.

What was never recorded is the geometry the compositor actually hands the lock
surface in the ext-session-lock configure event. If that width and height are
smaller than the panel, the client is drawing exactly what it was told to and
the defect is upstream of it; if they match, the loss is somewhere in this
clients own rendering path. One line of output separates those two cases, and
neither could be distinguished without it.

This is a diagnostic, not a fix, and it is deliberately kept: it costs one
fprintf per configure event on a surface that is configured once per lock, and
it goes to the same stderr log that 57-screensaver.sh already captures to
NCZ_LOCK_LOG.

RESULT, measured on the O6 with a payload built from this patch (bk8), by
exercising the lock against the greeter compositor on the real panel:

    lock: keymap accepted, keyboard ready
    lock: configure 3840x2160 scale=1 (buffer 3840x2160)

The compositor hands the lock surface the FULL panel resolution, the scale is
1, and the buffer is allocated at 3840x2160. So the surface is not configured
small, nothing is upscaled, and the client is drawing at native resolution.

That rules out the entire geometry-and-scale explanation -- which is what both
this patch and 0011 were written to investigate -- and means the reported
"lower resolution than the greeter" is NOT a resolution difference at the
Wayland protocol level. Whatever the eye is picking up is in what the lock
screen DRAWS (asset sizes, font choice and size, how the background is
composited) rather than in how many pixels it is given to draw into. Any
further work should start from a visual comparison of the two surfaces at
identical resolution, not from the protocol.

---
--- a/src/lockscreen/lock_main.c
+++ b/src/lockscreen/lock_main.c
@@ -354,6 +354,16 @@
                                    uint32_t serial, uint32_t w, uint32_t h) {
     struct lock_output *o = data;
     o->width = w; o->height = h; o->configured = true;
+    /* Log the geometry we are told to paint. On a 3840x2160 panel the lock
+     * screen was reported as visibly lower resolution than the greeter, and
+     * the obvious culprit -- a missing buffer scale -- turned out not to
+     * apply: the output reports SCALE = 1, measured with a read-only wl_output
+     * probe, so nothing is being upscaled. That leaves the configured size
+     * itself as the thing to check, and nothing recorded it. */
+    fprintf(stderr, "lock: configure %ux%u scale=%d (buffer %ux%u)\n",
+            w, h, o->scale > 0 ? o->scale : 1,
+            w * (unsigned)(o->scale > 0 ? o->scale : 1),
+            h * (unsigned)(o->scale > 0 ? o->scale : 1));
     ext_session_lock_surface_v1_ack_configure(s, serial);
     render_output(o);
 }
